{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795714488,"nonce":"TUDnGJRNHJQttOTXbrvVKSMrbwY=","title":"fem.solve with no linear= slot: four steady-solve branches hard-wire sparse_lu_solve instead of lu()'s preferred backend","message":"**What goes wrong.** `jno.solve.lu()` with no backend resolves late through `_preferred_direct_backend()`: cuDSS on a GPU in float64 when cuDSS is installed, else JAX `spsolve`. A steady linear `fem.solve()` with no `linear=` never asks it. Four branches of `FEM._solve_dispatch` (jno/_fem.py, the non-parametric steady linear case, around lines 4318-4355) call `sparse_lu_solve` (JAX `spsolve`, a sparse QR on the GPU) directly, whenever `solve_fn` is None:\n\n1. a periodic-tie problem: the reduced `P^T A P` system;\n2. a 1-D (LINE2) operator;\n3. any complex form (the real 2n block, so the n x n complex LDL^T of cuDSS is never reached);\n4. the Hermite / Argyris / Morley families (`_fem_prefer_direct`).\n\nRelated sites outside `_fem.py`: the default of `newton_direct` (#391), of `staggered` (#220), and the transient step in `solver_api.py` (`prefer_direct` without slots).\n\n**Reproduce.** Measured on a GPU in float64 with cuDSS installed, on main, counting calls to `linear.sparse_lu_solve`, `cudss_lu_solve` and `cudss_ldl_solve`:\n\n- 1-D Poisson on a line mesh, `fem.solve()`: 1 spsolve, 0 cuDSS. With `linear=jno.solve.lu()`: 0 spsolve, 1 cuDSS LDL^T.\n- Complex N1E cell (curl-curl, mass, first-order face term), `fem.solve()`: 1 spsolve, 0 cuDSS.\n\nCases 1 and 4 are read from the code, not run. The comparison with `lu()` on the complex cell is not clean on main: `lu()` fails its residual gate there, which is a separate fault.\n\n**Expected.** One definition of the default direct backend. A branch that wants a direct solve because Krylov does not converge (the comments say so) should take whatever `lu()` would pick, falling back to `sparse_lu_solve` only where no context exists.\n\n**Where.** jno/_fem.py `_solve_dispatch` (the four calls); `_default_direct` in the same file already resolves the cuDSS default for runtime-parametric, non-periodic forms and could be the single entry point.\n\n**Workaround.** Pass `linear=jno.solve.lu()` (or a named backend).\n\n**Direction.** Route the four branches through `_default_direct`-style resolution at `at_solve` time; extend it to the periodic and complex cases once the backend can take them. Test it on CPU by forcing the backend choice and spying on the solver functions.","files":null}]}